---
// The privacy page's section on recaps (#1384). Every sentence rests on the code (the pull request
// lists the lines): recaps/ and bot.py (what the AI is given, Post, the checks), recaps/notify.py
// (the email), campaigns/store.py (a recap goes with its campaign). It renders nothing until recaps
// are live (content/recap-words.ts), and the narration part nothing until audio is.
import { aiTrainingSentence } from "../content/legal";

interface Props {
  live: boolean;
  audio: boolean;
  share?: boolean;
}
const { live, audio, share = false } = Astro.props;
---

{
  live && (
    <>
      <h2 id="recaps">Recaps</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>What it is.</strong> After a session, DMbot can write a short story-style recap of
          it for the game master. Only paid plans have it.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Who writes it.</strong> The AI company named above (Anthropic). It gets the text of
          what people who said yes said in that session (marked only "DM" or "Player"), the names
          the game master confirmed (never the ones marked secret), the game's last posted recap, the
          day in the story, and anything the game master wrote for recaps: example recaps, style
          notes, and corrections typed for that recap. It does not get sound. {aiTrainingSentence}
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Who sees it.</strong> Only the game masters of that campaign, until one of them
          presses Post. Posting puts it in the table's chat channel (dmb-chat), where everyone who
          can see that channel can read it. DMbot emails a game master when it is ready or could
          not be written: the email has the title (or the reason) and a link, never the recap, and
          has no link to stop it. DMbot won't post a recap if a player has stopped being recorded
          since it was written.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>How long it is kept.</strong> A recap, and the list of what happened that it was
          made from, are kept with the campaign and deleted when the campaign is deleted. Trying
          again replaces the old text, and Don't post keeps the draft saved. We keep a short note
          that the email was sent, with no words in it, and delete it after 90 days. The email
          itself holds the recap's title and goes through Google, like our other email.
        </li>
        {share && (
          <li>
            <strong>A shared link.</strong> A game master can share a recap by a link. Anyone who
            has it can read the recap{audio ? " and play its voice and music" : ""} without signing
            in, even people outside your Discord server, and they can pass it on. We ask search
            engines not to list it, but that is only a request: treat the link like a password. A
            game master can turn a link off or make a new one. The old link then no longer opens
            the recap, though anyone who already saw it may keep a copy. A posted recap is already
            in the table's chat channel for everyone there. A recap that is not posted can be read
            only by the game masters of its campaign.
          </li>
        )}
        {audio && (
          <li>
            <strong>Narrated recaps.</strong> To make the voice and the music, the words to be read
            aloud and short descriptions of the music go to ElevenLabs. We do not keep the audio
            files on our server.
          </li>
        )}
      </ul>
    </>
  )
}
